<Project>

  <!--
    The trim and AOT analyzers, switched on where they mean something.

    They belong HERE and not in Directory.Build.props, and the difference is not
    cosmetic. Directory.Build.props is imported before the project body, so
    $(TargetFramework) is still empty when it is evaluated — and
    IsTargetFrameworkCompatible('', 'net8.0') is false. These three properties
    sat in that file with that condition and were never once set, in any
    project, for the life of the repo. Every claim in the README about nothing
    being discovered at run time was unverified, and the first thing switching
    them on found was fourteen violations in GatedFunction.cs, the file the
    README holds up as the reflection-free path.

    Directory.Build.targets is imported after the project body, so the condition
    is evaluated against a TargetFramework that exists.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsTargetFrameworkCompatible('$(TargetFramework)', 'net8.0'))
                            AND '$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Scoped to what ships. IsPackable is already false on the samples and on the
    test projects, and it is the right line rather than a convenient one: a
    library makes a promise to code it will never see, so its promise has to be
    checked. An application decides for itself at publish time, and a test
    assembly is never published at all — IL3000 on Assembly.Location in the
    generator harness describes a deployment that will not happen.

    A sample that wants the check can ask for it by setting the three
    properties itself.
  -->

</Project>
